{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Shall the axe boast.</b> An iron tool with which one cuts, and it is from the root נגזרתי [should read נגרזתי] <small>(Psalms 31:22)</small>. Now the meaning is that HaShem made him a metaphor, and 'mashor' has no meaning other than 'yishar' in Magra <small>(I Chronicles 20:3)</small>, and it is from double [root] formations, on the pattern of מעוז <small>(Isaiah 25:4)</small>:" "Another metaphor: 'as the staff waves, [so] those who lift it.' The meaning is like the waving of the staff and the wave-action of those who lift it, for the essence of the waving belongs to those who lift it: as one raises a staff, the raising belongs to the lifters, not to the wood."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Hebrew text contains נגזרתי marked with צ\"ל (correction should read) נגרזתי; translated the corrected form. 'Magra' refers to the Chronicles passage where the word appears; preserved Ibn Ezra's cross-reference as given.", 1 "Translated hyper-literally; Ibn Ezra's compressed wordplay on תנופה/הרמה/מרימים is preserved without expansion."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:53:14.655308211Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}